iagonal and a straight run.\n\n**Where the parts sit.** The fact sheet locates both parts in every pair.\n\n- **Straight run.** It always lies on a line through the cell that touches the 2 diagonally, on the side facing the 1. Call that cell T = 2 + (sign Δy, sign Δx).\n  - Pair 1: 2 at (1,6), 1 at (12,3), so T = (2,5). The run is column 5, rows 2..10.\n  - Pair 2: T = (2,2). The run is column 2, rows 2..7.\n  - Pair 3: 2 at (13,3), so T = (12,4). The run is column 4, rows 8..12.\n  - Pair 4: 2 at (3,11), so T = (4,10). The run is row 4, columns 7..10.\n- **Which way the run points.** It follows the longer axis of the separation.\n  - Pair 4 has |Δx| = 10 > |Δy| = 7, so its run is horizontal.\n  - Pairs 1–3 have |Δy| > |Δx|, so their runs are vertical.\n- **Diagonal.** It starts at the 1 and steps diagonally toward the 2. It stops when it reaches T's line on the short axis. That means T's column for a vertical run, or T's row for a horizontal one.\n  - Pair 1: (11,4), then (10,5), which reaches column 5.\n  - Pair 4: (9,2) … (4,7), which reaches row 4.\n- **Joining the parts.** From the diagonal's end, the path goes straight along the long axis until it reaches T.\n\n**Checking each pair.**\n\n- Pair 2: diagonal (12,7)…(7,2), then straight (6,2)…(2,2). Together this is exactly the set of changed cells.\n- Pair 3: diagonal (2,10)…(8,4), then straight (9,4)…(12,4). This matches.\n- Pairs 1 and 4 match through the same construction.\n\n**Test.** The 1 is at (1,4) and the 2 is at (13,12). Δy = 12 > Δx = 8, so the run is vertical, and T = (12,11).\n- Diagonal: (2,5), (3,6), … (8,11), which reaches column 11.\n- Straight: (9,11) … (12,11).\n\nThe program emits the canvas twice: once after the diagonal and once after the straight run.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid).copy()\n    p1 = tuple(np.argwhere(g == 1)[0])\n    p2 = tuple(np.argwhere(g == 2)[0])\n    dy, dx = p2[0] - p1[0], p2[1] - p1[1]\n    sy, sx = int(np.sign(dy)), int(np.sign(dx))\n    # target cell: diagonal neighbour of 2 facing the 1\n    T = (p2[0] - sy, p2[1] - sx)\n    vertical = abs(dy) >= abs(dx)\n    y, x = p1\n    # diagonal from 1 until aligned with T on the minor axis\n    while (x != T[1]) if vertical else (y != T[0]):\n        y += sy; x += sx\n        g[y, x] = 3\n    emit(g, \"Draw the diagonal from the 1 toward the 2 until it lines up with the cell diagonally next to the 2.\")\n    # straight run along the major axis up to T\n    while (y, x) != T:\n        if vertical:\n            y += sy\n        else:\n            x += sx\n        g[y, x] = 3\n    emit(g, \"Continue straight along the long axis until reaching the cell diagonally adjacent to the 2.\")\n    return g\n","candidate_tactics":{"paths":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Each output adds a path of colour 3 that joins them."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The path never covers either endpoint, and it always has two parts: a 45° diagonal and a straight run."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- **Joining the parts.** From the diagonal's end, the path goes straight along the long axis until it reaches T."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"f2d7bbb503e9364ca233242a83ba243704bcce314c811f5589c600108c647d60","task_sha256":"c72072f80a47de7a27712ce5046d79c6a6a3dc8e5e1ce2e501cae0f36ed20faf","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
